\section*{Question 3}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/bf65f69c-ff64-4bbf-b2da-7cafb9964822-1.jpg?height=290&width=756&top_left_y=397&top_left_x=670}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Graph of \(f\)}
\end{figure}
A continuous function \(f\) is defined on the closed interval \(-4 \leq x \leq 6\). The graph of \(f\) consists of a line segment and a curve that is tangent to the \(x\)-axis at \(x=3\), as shown in the figure above. On the interval \(0<x<6\), the function \(f\) is twice differentiable, with \(f^{\prime \prime}(x)>0\).
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Is \(f\) differentiable at \(x=0\) ? Use the definition of the derivative with one-sided limits to justify your answer.
\item[(b)] For how many values of \(a,-4 \leq a<6\), is the average rate of change of \(f\) on the interval \([a, 6]\) equal to 0 ? Give a reason for your answer.
\item[(c)] Is there a value of \(a,-4 \leq a<6\), for which the Mean Value Theorem, applied to the interval \([a, 6]\), guarantees a value \(c, a<c<6\), at which \(f^{\prime}(c)=\frac{1}{3}\) ? Justify your answer.
\item[(d)] The function \(g\) is defined by \(g(x)=\int_{0}^{x} f(t) d t\) for \(-4 \leq x \leq 6\). On what intervals contained in \([-4,6]\) is the graph of \(g\) concave up? Explain your reasoning.
\item[(a)] \(\lim _{h \rightarrow 0^{-}} \frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h}=\frac{2}{3}\)
\(\lim _{h \rightarrow 0^{+}} \frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h}<0\)
Since the one-sided limits do not agree, \(f\) is not differentiable at \(x=0\).
\item[(b)] \(\frac{f(6)-f(a)}{6-a}=0\) when \(f(a)=f(6)\). There are two values of \(a\) for which this is true.
\item[(c)] Yes, \(a=3\). The function \(f\) is differentiable on the interval \(3<x<6\) and continuous on \(3 \leq x \leq 6\).
Also, \(\frac{f(6)-f(3)}{6-3}=\frac{1-0}{6-3}=\frac{1}{3}\).
By the Mean Value Theorem, there is a value \(c\), \(3<c<6\), such that \(f^{\prime}(c)=\frac{1}{3}\).
\item[(d)] \(g^{\prime}(x)=f(x), g^{\prime \prime}(x)=f^{\prime}(x)\)
\(g^{\prime \prime}(x)>0\) when \(f^{\prime}(x)>0\)
This is true for \(-4<x<0\) and \(3<x<6\).
\end{itemize}

2 : \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}1 \text { : sets up difference quotient at } x=0 \\ 1 \text { : answer with justification }\end{array}\right.\)
\(2:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1 \text { : expression for average rate of change } \\ 1 \text { : answer with reason }\end{array}\right.\)
\(2:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: \text { answers "yes" and identifies } a=3 \\ 1: \text { justification }\end{array}\right.\)
\(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: g^{\prime}(x)=f(x) \\ 1: \text { considers } g^{\prime \prime}(x)>0 \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
